= Consciousness =

== Overview ==
'''Consciousness''' is the condition of having experience or being aware in a relevant sense. The term has multiple disciplinary meanings: subjective or ''phenomenal'' experience; availability of information to reasoning, report and behavioral control (''access consciousness''); levels of wakefulness or responsiveness; and self-awareness. These are related but '''not synonymous'''. The existence of human conscious experience does not settle the physical, computational or philosophical theory of consciousness.

== Phenomenology and access ==
Phenomenal consciousness concerns what an experience is like for a subject, including qualitative aspects often discussed as [[Qualia|qualia]]. Access consciousness concerns the availability of representations for flexible use, reports or control. Attention, reportability, [[Metacognition]], [[Memory]], and [[Cognition]] can correlate with aspects of consciousness; none is an uncontroversial necessary-and-sufficient test of all forms of experience.

== Scientific approaches ==
Neuroscience compares neural activity across wake, sleep, anesthesia, and controlled perceptual/report conditions. Proposed explanations include global neuronal workspace theory (widespread availability/broadcast), recurrent processing, higher-order theories and integrated information theory. They make overlapping but different predictions and use non-equivalent operationalizations.

A preregistered 2025 adversarial collaboration directly compared global neuronal workspace and integrated-information predictions. The results challenged important predictions of both frameworks without identifying a consensus winner. See [https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-025-08888-1 published study].

== Computation and agents ==
A system may process information, maintain state, act on goals, communicate, or construct a self-model without this sufficing to establish phenomenal experience. [[AI agent]], [[Self-modeling agent]], and [[Agent self-identity]] describe technical or functional properties. Whether, and under what conditions, computational architectures could support phenomenal experience remains disputed. Physical implementation, cognitive function, first-person experience and social attribution must not be collapsed.

== Biological perspective ==
Organisms differ in their nervous systems, sensory capacities and behaviors. Measures appropriate to one species do not automatically transfer to another. Simple signal integration or cellular information processing does not by itself demonstrate consciousness in a bacterium; equally, lack of human language is not by itself evidence of lack of experience.

== Epistemic notes ==
First-person evidence and third-person measurement have different access conditions. Empirical research can constrain theory, but objective markers of experience, philosophical interpretation and machine-consciousness claims remain active research problems. An information-theoretic statistic such as [[Entropy]] does not alone diagnose consciousness.

